! mcp_bootstrap_p195.strap -- MCP printed page 195: the tail of the
! "rewrite the label" arm, the scratch-tape test, the three eight-word
! operator messages (REPLACE THE REEL / INSERT A RING / REPLACE THE
! REEL ... MCP REEL), and the tape-label control-word block
! (JUNLDK / JRDLK / JLABIN / JERGK / JWKOM) that ends the LG deck.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.202.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 and 1200 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID
! 11D11MCP   CARD ID LG000318   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER
! 195".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 221651. <<<
! Six digits, printed after the word LOCATION at the right-hand end
! of the heading line, clear of the dashed rule.  That is LINE 1'S
! location word (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and line 1
! here IS a located row -- the case the rule predicts to be NUMBERED
! rather than blank.  Line 1's own LOCATION column reads 221651.40.
! Two independent witnesses for the origin word; the heading gives no
! bit offset, so the ".40" rests on the LOCATION column and on the
! join (below), which pins it twice more.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-SIX PRINTED LINES.  FORTY-ONE COMPILE (lines 1-27, 29,
! 30, 32, 33, 35, 36, 38-45), FOUR ARE "CNOP" CARDS THAT EMIT
! NOTHING HERE (28, 31, 34, 37 -- the counter is already word
! aligned at each of them), AND ONE IS A COMMENT CARD (line 46,
! printed "--" in the NAME column, the LG deck's last card).  No
! SLC, no SYN, no VF, no CF.  ONE RESERVATION: line 45's
! "DRZ(N),(3)".
!
! THE ACCOUNTING, so the number above is self-checking: 41 rows
! occupy storage -- 34 print a BINARY OUTPUT and all 34 carry a
! !CHECK, 6 are "(IQS*)DD" cards that print no binary, and 1 is the
! reservation.  41 + 4 CNOPs + 1 comment card = 46 printed lines.
!
! The page is CONTIGUOUS from 221651.40 to 221732.77 -- the three
! words 221730-221732 being the DRZ -- and then blank paper to the
! foot of the form.  No hole; the counter never jumps.
!
!   lines  1-12   the tail of the rewrite-label arm: B,JTRYLG, the
!                 LVE/SV pair that plants the rewrite-label command
!                 word's chain address in J WKOM+1.0, LX,$0,JWKOM,
!                 the reject exit (SIC,KKRET / B,KIOREJ), the
!                 LVI,$1,$+1.0 / B,JSEOPT linkage, the four-way
!                 return fan (NOP / NOP / B,JWTERR) and line 12's
!                 TI,3,JPREVX,$1 normal return.
!   lines 13-21   JWRITX (B,$ -- a wait loop), JWTERR's retry
!                 countdown through JBACKT, JERRD's backspace
!                 (SIC,JBSPX / B,JBSPE), the second countdown to
!                 JRDLAB and JNEUT's new-tape call (SIC,JNEWT /
!                 B,JNEWTE / B,JUNRL).
!   lines 22-27   JBDLAB, the scratch-tape test: LV,$2,SCREEL($12)
!                 picks up the reel, LF(BU,30) loads the current
!                 reel name, BB,SOWNER($11) tests the owner bit,
!                 BZRZ,JNEUT gives up, SF(BU,30),SSCRPM($12) files
!                 the name in the UAT and B,JRWRIT rewrites.
!   lines 28-37   three EIGHT-WORD operator messages in IQS
!                 characters, each a CNOP and two "(IQS*)DD(BU),"
!                 cards: JREPSC/JREPCU, JINSM/JINSCU,
!                 JREPCT/JRCTCU.
!   lines 38-45   the tape-label I/O block: JUNLDK (unload, control
!                 code 0o137), JRDLK (read label) with its control
!                 word at 221723.00, JLABIN (the label slot, an XW),
!                 JERGK (erase long gap, control code 0o56), JWKOM
!                 (write label) with its control word at 221727.00,
!                 and JPREVX, three words reserved for index
!                 registers.
!   line  46      a comment card -- "--" in the NAME column, nothing
!                 else on the line.  printed p.194 line 2 prints the
!                 identical card directly after ITS "DRZ(N),(3)".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SPAN, ESTABLISHED FROM THE PAGE (NOT FROM THE SURVEY)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The brief no longer quotes survey endpoints and this deck did not
! consult them.  Three independent readings of the page agree:
!
!   * THE LINE COLUMN runs 1..46 with no break.  Line 1 is located
!     (221651.40); line 45 is located (221730.00); line 46 is a
!     comment card; nothing is printed below line 46 but the
!     closing dashed rule.
!   * THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN runs LG000318 (line 1, which is also
!     the header's CARD ID) to LG000362 (line 46).  Forty-five
!     distinct serials over forty-six lines: LG000319 appears TWICE,
!     on lines 2 and 3, which is the two-statement card
!     "LVE,0,RV4 <sep> SV,0,J WKOM+1.0" -- line 3 is printed
!     INDENTED TWO CHARACTER POSITIONS, which is where the statement
!     separator stood on the card.  Nothing fell off either end.
!   * THE COUNTER CHAIN closes on itself: every located row follows
!     from the one above at half-word or full-word granularity, and
!     the three message blocks each come to exactly 64 IQS
!     characters = 8 words, which is what the "- 8 WORD MESSAGE"
!     comments assert (see THE MESSAGES).
!
!   first location  221651.40   (line 1)
!   last location   221730.00   (line 45, a 3-word reservation)
!   first free word 221733.00
!
! >>> LG000317 PRINTS NO LISTING LINE. <<<  printed p.194's last
! line is its line 50, LG000316, at 221651.00; this page's first is
! LG000318.  The brief records two other serials that print no line
! (ED000049, IH000041), both at page breaks; this is a third, and it
! is at a page break too.  Nothing is missing from the counter: p.194
! line 50 is a half word at 221651.00, so the counter arrives here at
! 221651.40, which is exactly line 1's location.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, AT BOTH ENDS, READ OFF THE NEIGHBOURING PAGES
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BACKWARD.  printed p.194 (PDF p.201) line 50 is
! "221651.00  000000.30 00   NOP", a half word, so the counter
! arrives at 221651.40.  This page's line 1 is at 221651.40.  The
! two agree to the bit.
!
! AND THE SAME FOUR CARDS ARE PRINTED ON BOTH PAGES, which is a
! stronger check than the join alone.  printed p.194's lines 44-47
! are "JTRYLG LX,$0,JERGK" / "SIC,KKRET" / "B,KIOREJ" /
! "LVI,$1,$+1.0", and this page's lines 4-7 are the same four cards
! with JWKOM for JERGK.  Three of the four words are IDENTICAL
! (221512.40 80, 221505.10 00, and the LVI's byte), which is what
! settles two glyphs on this page -- see GLYPH CALLS 1 and 2.
!
! FORWARD.  printed p.196 (PDF p.203) opens with a three-line
! asterisk banner (CARD ID LH000001 -- A NEW DECK; this page's
! LG000362 is the LG deck's last card) and its line 4 is
! "221733.00  222017.44 30  K UNLOD  LV,2,K UL MES".  This page ends
! with a three-word reservation at 221730.00, so the next free word
! is 221733.00.  The two agree exactly.  The forward join is
! therefore READ, not asserted.
!
! >>> AND THE ORIGIN IS CHECKED BY THE MERGE, NOT MERELY ASSERTED.
! <<< The page prints no SLC, so this deck writes a hard
! "SLC,(8)221651.40".  Per docs/transcription_workflow.md that would
! normally be an assertion -- but two landed decks already SYN
! labels this page DEFINES:
!
!   decks/mcp_bootstrap_p189.strap:  JWRITX SYN,(8)221660.0   (line 8
!       "K LABEL SIC,JWRITX" -- a SIC, so BIT-EXACT)
!   decks/mcp_bootstrap_p190.strap:  JWRITX SYN,(8)221660.00
!                                    JPREVX SYN,(8)221730.00
!
! Neither is a SYN alias of another symbol -- both are hard octal
! constants read off a printed instruction -- so the false positive
! printed p.189 found does not apply here.  THE INJECTION WAS RUN,
! not reasoned about: merging pp.99-192 plus this deck succeeds (841
! defined, 61 external), and moving this deck's SLC from 221651.40
! to 221652.00 makes mcp_image.merge refuse --
!
!   FAIL JPREVX: printed p.190 SYNs bit 4781568,
!                printed p.195 places it at bit 4781632
!
! THE ORIGIN IS CHECKED, not asserted.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE MESSAGES -- AND WHY THEIR LENGTHS ARE A READING
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Lines 29, 30, 32, 33, 35 and 36 are "(IQS*)DD(BU), TEXT *" cards.
! They print NOTHING in the BINARY OUTPUT column (the same thing
! printed p.21's IQS messages do), so no !CHECK can test their
! bits.  What DOES test them is the LOCATION of the card below:
! each datum's length in characters is pinned to the character by
! the next printed location, and this deck's !SYM rows check all
! six.
!
! The trailing blanks inside a datum are DATA (the workflow's "in
! entry mode the space after the comma is DATA"), so they were
! measured, not guessed.  Two independent methods agree on all six:
!
!   (a) ARITHMETIC off the printed LOCATION column --
!         221674.50 - 221671.00 = 232 bits = 29 characters
!         221701.00 - 221674.50 = 280 bits = 35 characters
!         221704.20 - 221701.00 = 208 bits = 26 characters
!         221711.00 - 221704.20 = 304 bits = 38 characters
!         221714.50 - 221711.00 = 232 bits = 29 characters
!         221721.00 - 221714.50 = 280 bits = 35 characters
!       and each pair sums to 512 bits = 8 words, which is what the
!       "- 8 WORD MESSAGE" comment on lines 28, 31 and 34 says.
!   (b) INK-SEGMENTED CHARACTER CELLS at 400 dpi.  The statement
!       column is 10 characters to the inch, so every glyph's cell
!       index is measurable.  The terminating "*" lands on cell 42,
!       48, 39, 51, 42 and 48 respectively, counting the "(" of
!       "(IQS*)" as cell 0; the datum starts at cell 13, right after
!       the comma.  Same six lengths.
!
! So line 30 carries FIVE trailing blanks after "TAPE." and line 33
! carries NINE after "UNIT.", and lines 29, 32 and 35 carry ONE
! leading blank after the comma and ONE trailing.  Line 36 carries
! none at either end -- its "REEL*" is flush.  Lines 29 and 35 are
! the SAME CARD printed twice and measure identically (terminator on
! cell 42 both times), which is the page checking itself.
!
! >>> A MODE-ONLY dds ON AN ENTRY-MODE DD TAKES ITS LENGTH FROM THE
! DATUM. <<<  The brief leaves open whether "(BU)" with no length
! inherits one; these six cards are a measurement of the DD case,
! though not of the brief's case (which is about a dds-less
! statement taking a length from its operand SYMBOL).  Written as
! printed, "(IQS*)DD(BU), REPLACE THE REEL ON CHANNEL *" compiles to
! 232 bits and every one of the six message labels lands on its
! printed location.  Had strap_asm treated "(BU)" as an explicit
! length 0 (effective 64) here, JREPCU would have come out at
! 221672.00 instead of 221674.50 and five more labels after it would
! have moved.  They do not.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- every one is a stand-in, none is a copied card
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Sixteen symbols are referenced and not defined here.  TWELVE are
! already landed with the SAME value and the SAME spelling, so this
! deck's SYN is CHECKED against them by the merge:
!
!   RV4     221201.0    landed pp.187/190
!   KKRET   221512.40   landed; SIC on line 5 -- BIT-EXACT
!   KIOREJ  221505.0    landed; B on line 6 shows bit 32 only
!   JSEOPT  221525.0    landed; defined on printed p.192 line 27
!   JBSPX   221520.40   landed; SIC on line 16 -- BIT-EXACT
!   JBSPE   221514.0    landed; B on line 17 shows bit 32 only
!   JNEWT   221614.0    landed; SIC on line 19 -- BIT-EXACT
!   JNEWTE  221541.0    landed; defined on printed p.192 line 51
!   JRWRIT  221644.40   landed; B on line 27 prints 221644.50
!   SCREEL  SYN(BU,18), 8.32   landed on four decks, dds carried
!   SOWNER  SYN(BU,1), 0.32    landed on three decks, dds carried
!   SSCRPM  SYN(BU,64), 6.0    landed on four decks, dds carried
!
! FOUR are not yet landed; all four are defined on printed p.194,
! whose LOCATION column was read directly for this deck (PDF p.201):
!
!   JTRYLG  221646.0    p.194 line 44's label, at 221646.00
!   JBACKT  221645.0    p.194 line 42's label, at 221645.00
!   JRDLAB  221636.0    p.194, inferred from THIS page's line 18
!                       printing 221636.06 (a CB shows bit 32 only)
!   JUNRL   221634.40   from THIS page's line 21 printing 221634.50
!
! THE THREE dds-CARRYING EXTERNALS ARE COPIED FROM THE DECKS THAT
! LAND THEM, NOT INVENTED.  SCREEL, SOWNER and SSCRPM are each
! written by a VFL or index operation here with NO dds of its own
! (lines 22, 24, 26), so the length comes from the symbol and the
! merge refuses a disagreement.  Conversely nothing here declares a
! dds that no page prints -- the brief's printed p.189 case.
!
! BIT OFFSETS THAT ARE NOT OBSERVABLE.  KIOREJ, JBSPE, JSEOPT,
! JNEWTE, JTRYLG, JBACKT, JRDLAB and JUNRL are reached only by a B
! or a CB, which show BIT 32 and nothing below it (the workflow's
! three tiers).  Their ".0"/".40" is READ to the half word and
! CONVENTION below it.  KKRET, JBSPX and JNEWT are reached by a SIC
! and are bit-exact.  RV4 is reached by LVE; SCREEL by LV; both are
! index operations and show bit 32 only -- but SCREEL, SOWNER and
! SSCRPM are displacements landed by other decks and are not read
! here at all.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS -- the round-glyph and B/8 decisions, with evidence
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This drum opens round glyphs constantly and the workflow's
! measurement says neither width nor openness separates O from 0
! from C.  Every call below is settled by another page, by the octal
! field, or by what produced the byte -- never by shape alone.
!
! (1) LINE 7'S BYTE IS 01, NOT C1.  The drum prints "221655.43 C1".
!     printed p.194 line 47 is the IDENTICAL CARD, "LVI,$1,$+1.0",
!     and prints "221650.43 01" with a closed glyph.  One card, two
!     pages, one drum: the C is a damaged 0.
!
! (2) LINE 2'S BYTE IS B0, NOT 80 -- AND THIS ONE WENT THE OTHER
!     WAY.  At 400 dpi it reads "80" and the first draft of this
!     deck recorded that.  At 1200 dpi the first glyph has a
!     STRAIGHT VERTICAL LEFT STEM and squared top-left corners,
!     while line 5's and line 12's known 80s are round on both
!     bowls; line 24's "8B" prints the two shapes side by side in
!     one byte and they are plainly different.  strap_asm compiles
!     LVE,0,RV4 to B0 independently.  B vs 8 IS separable on this
!     drum at 1200 dpi, unlike the round glyphs.
!
! (3) LINE 12'S BYTE IS A0, NOT AC.  The drum prints "000021.C6 AC".
!     The octal field ".C6" cannot hold a C, so that row demonstrably
!     drops ink on round glyphs (rung 5), and the final glyph has the
!     same open shape as that proven 0.  What settles it is the
!     statement: TI's low nibble is the SECOND operand's index, and
!     "$1" carries none, so the nibble is 0.  printed p.192 line 51
!     measured the same instruction the same way at 1200 dpi.
!
! (4) LINE 22'S BYTE REALLY IS 3C.  Opposite call to (3), same page,
!     which is why no blanket round-glyph rule was applied here:
!     "LV,$2,SCREEL($12)" DOES carry an index on its operand, $12 =
!     0xC, so the low nibble is C.  Likewise line 24's 8B ($11),
!     line 26's 8C ($12) and lines 38/39/42/43's 8E ($14).
!
! (5) LINE 25'S BYTE IS C0.  "BZRZ,JNEUT" compiles C0; the leading
!     glyph is a genuine C (the BZ branch code) and the trailing one
!     a genuine 0.  Recorded because the row looks exactly like the
!     damaged rows above and is not one.
!
! (6) THE OCTAL FIELDS ".C6" (lines 12, 14, 18, 23) AND ".CC"
!     THROUGHOUT are .06 and .00 -- a C cannot occur in an octal
!     field.  Lines 14 and 18 are "CB,$3,<label>": 0o06 = 2J for
!     J=3 with bit 32 clear, which is what the CB encoding requires,
!     so the arithmetic agrees with the octal-field argument.
!
! (7) "J WKOM" / "JWKOM" ON CONSECUTIVE CARDS.  Line 3 prints
!     "J WKOM+1.0" with a blank inside the name; line 4 prints
!     "JWKOM" without one, and line 43's NAME field prints "JWKOM".
!     They are the same symbol: line 3 compiles to 221727.00, which
!     is line 43's 221726.00 plus one word.  Blanks in a STRAP name
!     are not significant; the page simply punched it both ways.
!
! (8) "DRZ" ON LINE 45 IS READ OFF THE INK, as the workflow
!     requires: a reservation prints its COUNT, so no run can tell
!     DR from DRZ.  At 900 dpi the Z is unambiguous, and printed
!     p.194 line 1 prints the identical card "JNEWXR DRZ(N),(3)".
!
! (9) "JWKOM"'S FOURTH GLYPH IS AN O, AND THE ARGUMENT IS
!     STRUCTURAL, NOT AN INK LEVEL.  The name is printed three
!     times (lines 3, 4, 43) and nothing outside this page
!     references it, so a wrong letter here would be invisible to
!     every check -- the exact hazard the workflow describes.
!     Segmented at 1800 dpi against the KNOWN C and KNOWN O of the
!     same row's comment "-WRITE LABEL COMMAND":
!
!       known C   right side ABSENT for thirteen consecutive rows
!       known O   right side PRESENT, with dropout
!       the name's glyph   right side PRESENT, with dropout
!
!     Dropout opens a closed glyph and never closes an open one, so
!     a glyph with a right stroke CANNOT be a C.  That leaves O or
!     the digit 0, which this drum does not separate by shape at
!     all; "JWK0M" is not a plausible name, so O.  RESIDUAL RISK:
!     O-versus-0, low, and unresolvable from this page.
!
! (10) "JERRD" (line 16) AND "JRDLK" (line 39) END AND CARRY A D,
!     not an O.  At 1800 dpi both glyphs have the straight vertical
!     left stem and squared corners the workflow names as the
!     discriminator; a C or an O is round with no stem.  Recorded
!     because the volume OCR reads them "JERRO"/"JERRE" and
!     "JROLK" -- the D/O confusion the brief warns is not in the
!     spelling checker's confusable set.  The OCR is the same ink
!     read by a worse reader and is not a witness against the crop.
!     Likewise line 18's "JRDLAB" and line 22's "JBDLAB" are
!     different symbols, not one misread twice: line 18 compiles
!     221636.06 and line 22's label is at 221664.40.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SIX LABELS AND TWO EXTERNALS HAVE A WITNESS ON ANOTHER PAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt is an OCR of this listing, so its
! reading of printed p.195 is NOT a witness for printed p.195.  Its
! reading of printed pp.193-194 is a different page, and those lines
! (OCR lines 9993-10065, all before line 10078 which is this page's
! line 1) independently print six of this page's labels and the
! value of two of its externals:
!
!   JREPCU   "221601.00  221674.50+  VF JREPCU"      -- a VF prints
!            its 24-bit value, so 221674.50 BIT-EXACT
!   JRCTCU   "221615.40  221714.50+  VF,JRCTCU"      -- ditto
!   JLABIN   "221642.00  221724.00 80 036000.06 70
!             LF(BU,30),JLABIN -IS THIS THE SAME LABEL"
!            -- a VFL OPERAND reads every bit: 221724.00 BIT-EXACT
!   JINSM    "221633.40  221701.00 80 ... JINSM"
!   JREPCT   "221616.00  221711.05 01  LVI,$2,JREPCT"
!   JUNLDK   "221721.00 10  JTAKF LX,$0,JUNLDK -UNLOAD COMMAND"
!            and again at "221626.40 ... JEPGKF LX,$0,JUNLDK"
!   JUNRL    DEFINED there -- "221634.40  221325.40 80  JUNRL
!            SIC,KUNRR -WAIT FOR UNIT TO" -- which confirms this
!            deck's SYN at 221634.40 exactly, not merely to the
!            half word
!   JTRYLG   "221646.00  221725.00 10  JTRYLG LX,$0,JERGK -ERASE
!            LONG GAP" -- confirms 221646.00 and JERGK at 221725.00
!
! So three of this page's labels are pinned BIT-EXACTLY by another
! page's VF or VFL operand before pp.193-194 are even transcribed.
! JREPSC, JERRD, JBDLAB, JRDLK and JWKOM have NO witness anywhere
! and rest on the ink alone.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! DEPARTURES FROM THE PRINTED FORM -- TWO, BOTH FLAGGED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The brief's rule is to hand strap_asm the printed form and let a
! bad row FAIL.  Two rows on this page cannot fail, because
! strap_asm raises and assembles NOTHING -- the duplicate-label
! situation in another costume -- so the whole page would check
! nothing.  Both are recorded here and in the statement text.
!
! (A) LINE 3 IS PRINTED "SV,0,J WKOM+1.0" AND IS WRITTEN HERE
!     "SV,0,JWKOM+1.0".  A blank inside a name INSIDE AN EXPRESSION
!     is refused -- "line N: undefined symbol 'J'" -- which the
!     brief already lists as a known gap ("K WS.19", "K GOOF+.32").
!     The bare blanked name resolves; only the expression form does
!     not.  THE COMPILED WORD IS UNAFFECTED: the symbol is the same
!     one line 4 and line 43 spell without the blank, and the row's
!     !CHECK tests the printed 221727.01 30.
!
! (B) LINE 44 IS PRINTED "CW,SSCRPM($12),1.0" AND IS WRITTEN HERE
!     "CW,SSCRPM,1.0".  strap_asm's CW evaluates its ADDRESS field
!     with eval_expr, which refuses an index -- "cannot parse
!     expression 'SSCRPM($12)' at '($12)'".  THE COMPILED WORD IS
!     UNAFFECTED AND THAT IS DEMONSTRABLE, not assumed: a control
!     word has no index field at all (C28-6129 printed p.14 -- 18-bit
!     ADDRESS, three flag bits, an 18-bit COUNT and an 18-bit CHAIN
!     ADDRESS), and the page itself prints the flag group as "000".
!     The form written here compiles to "000006.00+ 000 000001
!     000000", character for character what the page prints.
!
! Everything else is the printed spelling verbatim and strap_asm
! accepted it, including the mode-only "(IQS*)DD(BU)," entry-mode
! cards with their embedded blanks, "LF(BU,30),.34($2),30" with a
! bare-decimal bit offset, "BB,SOWNER($11),$+1.32", "BZRZ",
! "DRZ(N),(3)", the "(SEOP)" modifier, the control codes written as
! addresses ("(8)137.0", "(8)56.0"), and the bare index number of
! "TI,3,JPREVX,$1".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK -- AND A SECOND, DIFFERENT MARK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Recorded, with NO rule proposed from one page (printed p.147
! proposed one and printed p.151 refuted it the same afternoon).
! The positions were MEASURED, by ink-segmenting the location column
! at 400 dpi across all forty-six rows, not eyeballed:
!
!   * THE FAMILIAR TRAILING MARK -- two character cells to the RIGHT
!     of the location -- is on FIVE rows: lines 21 (221664.00,
!     B,JUNRL), 30 (221674.50, JREPCU), 35 (221711.00, JREPCT),
!     43 (221726.00, JWKOM) and 45 (221730.00, JPREVX).
!   * >>> LINE 44 CARRIES A DIFFERENT MARK: AN ASTERISK IMMEDIATELY
!     LEFT OF THE LOCATION, in the cell lines 42/43/45 leave blank,
!     and it has NO trailing mark. <<<  So the listing prints the
!     line number's own asterisk, then a second one, then the
!     digits: "44**221727.00".  Every other row on this page prints
!     a blank there.  printed p.191 saw something "just left of its
!     location" on its line 36 and called it dirt; this one is a
!     full asterisk the same size as the others, so the two columns
!     are probably real and distinct.  OFFERED AS AN OBSERVATION.
!   * Line 1 carries a SPECK about one cell right of its location,
!     much smaller than an asterisk and with no arms.  Called dirt.
!
! WHAT THE TRAILING MARK IS NOT, ON THIS PAGE: it is not the
! mnemonic (lines 29/30/32/33/35/36 are all the same "(IQS*)DD"
! card and only two carry it); it is not "has a label" (lines 13,
! 14, 16, 19, 22, 29, 32, 33, 36, 38, 39, 41, 42 are labelled and
! carry none); it is not "the first card of a two-statement card"
! (lines 2 and 3 are the only such pair here and neither carries
! one); it is not a branch target inside the page (JNEUT on line 19
! is branched to twice and carries none).  The page that would kill
! any rule drawn from the LEADING mark is whichever neighbour prints
! both kinds on one row.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT IS CHECKED AND WHAT IS NOT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! !SYM covers all seventeen labels this page defines, against the
! printed LOCATION column.  !CHECK covers all thirty-one rows that
! print a BINARY OUTPUT: lines 1-27 and 38-44.
!
! NOT CHECKED, and the reasons:
!   * lines 28, 31, 34, 37 (CNOP) print no location and no binary.
!   * lines 29, 30, 32, 33, 35, 36 print no binary (an IQS DD does
!     not).  Their LENGTHS are checked, through the !SYM of the
!     label below each -- see THE MESSAGES.  >>> THEIR CONTENT IS
!     CHECKED BY NOTHING. <<<  192 characters of IQS text go into
!     the merged image and no test reads one of them: a misread
!     letter inside a word keeps the length identical and every
!     check still passes.  Two things bound it and neither is a
!     test -- the text is ENGLISH, so a misspelling is visible to a
!     reader, and lines 29 and 35 are the same card printed twice
!     and were measured to the same terminator cell.  What would
!     close it is another page quoting one of these messages, or a
!     tape image.
!   * line 45's "000003.00" is the RESERVATION COUNT, not storage,
!     so the oracle cannot see it (workflow, WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT
!     SEE item 5); pp.108/109/114 give their DRZ rows no !CHECK for
!     the same reason.  Whether those three words are DR (nothing)
!     or DRZ (zeros) is settled only by the ink -- glyph call 8.
!   * line 46 is a comment card.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

